    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d724">

                <pb facs="0689" n="677" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg677"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.62/819: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-29T16:00:00+09:00">November 29,
                            1940—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 29—11:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1228. On the principle <hi rendition="#italic">in vino Veritas</hi> the following
                    recent observations of a prominent member of the staff of the Soviet Embassy,
                    generally believed to be the local representative of OGPU,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d724-f1"/> made during a somewhat “wet” gathering, are reported for
                    what they may be worth. This information comes from a friendly diplomat.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Soviet Russia will never tie her hands by entering any agreement with any
                        other country or group of countries except on the broadest and most general
                        terms and this applies equally to Germany and Japan.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>An independent China is a fundamental principle in Soviet Russia’s Asiatic
                        policy and no agreement with Japan which might invalidate that principle
                        would be possible.</item>
                </list>
                <p>From another usually reliable informant we are told that in reply to an informal
                    suggestion made by Japanese representatives in Moscow, the Soviet Government has
                    flatly refused to discuss with Japan the relations between Russia and any third
                    country, and has declined to discuss the question of Russian assistance to
                    Chiang Kai-shek. The same source states that German representatives in Moscow
                    have not been permitted to take part in the Russo-Japanese conversations
                    there.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d724-f1" n="1" xml:id="d724-f1-content">
